## Proof

Let $G = \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Q}$ be the quotient of the additive group $\mathbb{R}$ by its subgroup $\mathbb{Q}$. The elements of $G$ are the cosets $x + \mathbb{Q}$ for $x \in \mathbb{R}$, where $x + \mathbb{Q} = y + \mathbb{Q}$ iff $x - y \in \mathbb{Q}$. Thus $\mathbb{R}$ is partitioned into cosets, each of cardinality $|\mathbb{Q}| = \aleph_{0}$ (countably infinite). Let $\kappa = |G|$ denote the number of distinct cosets.

**Upper bound.** The map $\varphi: \mathbb{R} \to G$ sending $x \mapsto x + \mathbb{Q}$ is surjective, so
$$\kappa = |G| \le |\mathbb{R}| = 2^{\aleph_{0}}.$$

**Lower bound.** Since $\mathbb{R}$ is the disjoint union of $\kappa$ cosets, each of size $\aleph_{0}$, cardinal arithmetic gives
$$|\mathbb{R}| = \kappa \cdot \aleph_{0}.$$
That is,
$$2^{\aleph_{0}} = \kappa \cdot \aleph_{0}.$$

Suppose, for contradiction, that $\kappa \le \aleph_{0}$ (i.e. $G$ is at most countable). Then $\kappa \cdot \aleph_{0} \le \aleph_{0} \cdot \aleph_{0} = \aleph_{0}$, which would force $|\mathbb{R}| = 2^{\aleph_{0}} \le \aleph_{0}$, contradicting Cantor's theorem that $\mathbb{R}$ is uncountable. Hence $\kappa > \aleph_{0}$; the quotient is uncountable.

With $\kappa$ an uncountable (hence infinite) cardinal, the product rule for infinite cardinals yields
$$\kappa \cdot \aleph_{0} = \max(\kappa, \aleph_{0}) = \kappa.$$
Combining with $2^{\aleph_{0}} = \kappa \cdot \aleph_{0}$, we obtain
$$\kappa = 2^{\aleph_{0}}.$$

Therefore the quotient group $\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Q}$ has exactly $2^{\aleph_{0}}$ distinct elements — the cardinality of the continuum (uncountably many, in bijection with $\mathbb{R}$ itself).

